Repair notes (rebuttals): Major issue (two defensible answers): I took the first suggested option. Both prompts now state that the SPA must send the cookie, so `AllowCredentials()` is a required part of the policy. That leaves only line 19 as the defect. bugLineSets is now [[19]]; the [19, 22] set is dropped because line 22 is declared necessary. The answer and explanation say why line 22 is not the defect. Line numbers were recounted: line 19 is `policy.SetIsOriginAllowed(_ => true)` and line 22 is `.AllowCredentials();`.; Unsure issue (framework behaviour): I removed the claim that ASP.NET Core 'does not allow' `AllowAnyOrigin()` together with `AllowCredentials()`. I recall that `CorsPolicyBuilder.Build` throws for this combination, but the docs page describes it differently, and neither is in the excerpt. The explanation now relies only on two statements in the fetched excerpt. First, 'Allowing cross-origin credentials is a security risk.' Second, 'The CORS specification also states that setting origins to "*" (all origins) is invalid if the Access-Control-Allow-Credentials header is present.' The origin reflection is supported by the preflight example in the same excerpt: the request has `Origin: https://cors1.azurewebsites.net` and the response has `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://cors1.azurewebsites.net` with `Vary: Origin`. That a predicate returning true leads to the request origin being echoed comes from my recall of `CorsService`, held with high but not verified certainty.; Minor issues fixed. The RU explanation now uses 'Предварительный запрос (preflight)', which clears the parity warning. Both locales now say the attack needs a browser that sends third-party cookies and that some browsers, for example Safari, block them by default. Naming Safari as blocking third-party cookies by default is from recall.
